                "content": "**Notation used throughout**\n\n*\u202f\\(E^{\\circ}\\) \u2013 standard reduction potential (V)  \n*\u202f\\(n\\) \u2013 number of electrons transferred in the half\u2011reaction as written  \n*\u202f\\(F\\) \u2013 Faraday constant (96485\u202fC\u202fmol\u207b\u00b9)  \n*\u202f\\(E\\) \u2013 cell potential under non\u2011standard conditions (V)  \n*\u202f\\(Q\\) \u2013 reaction quotient  \n\nThe activities of pure solids (\\(XO_{2}(s)\\)) and of liquid water are taken as unity.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (1)  Overall five\u2011electron reduction of \\(XO_{4}^{-}\\) to \\(X^{2+}\\)\n\nAdd the two given half\u2011reactions:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{(i)}&\\quad XO_{4}^{-}+4\\,H^{+}+3e^{-}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;XO_{2}(s)+2\\,H_{2}O &&E_{1}^{\\circ}=0.900\\ {\\rm V}\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{(ii)}&\\quad XO_{2}(s)+4\\,H^{+}+2e^{-}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;X^{2+}+2\\,H_{2}O &&E_{2}^{\\circ}=1.200\\ {\\rm V}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding (i) and (ii) eliminates the intermediate \\(XO_{2}(s)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{XO_{4}^{-}+8\\,H^{+}+5e^{-}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;X^{2+}+4\\,H_{2}O}\n\\tag{overall}\n\\]\n\nThe standard Gibbs energy change of a half\u2011reaction is \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}=-nF E^{\\circ}\\).  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Delta G^{\\circ}_{\\rm overall}\n &=\\Delta G^{\\circ}_{(i)}+\\Delta G^{\\circ}_{(ii)}\n   =-(3F)(0.900)- (2F)(1.200)\\\\\n &=-2.700F-2.400F=-5.100F .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nWith \\(n_{\\rm overall}=5\\),\n\n\\[\nE^{\\circ}_{\\rm overall}= -\\frac{\\Delta G^{\\circ}_{\\rm overall}}{nF}\n                         =\\frac{5.100F}{5F}= \\boxed{1.020\\ {\\rm V}} .\n\\]\n\n**Why the simple arithmetic mean is wrong**\n\nIf one simply averaged the two potentials,\n\\((0.900+1.200)/2=1.050\\ {\\rm V}\\), the result would be larger than the true value.  \nThe correct weighting must reflect the number of electrons that each step contributes,\nbecause \\(\\Delta G^{\\circ}=-nF E^{\\circ}\\) is linear in \\(n\\).  \nConsequently the overall potential is the **electron\u2011weighted average**, not the un\u2011weighted mean.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (2)  Is \\(XO_{2}(s)\\) thermodynamically unstable toward disproportionation?\n\nDisproportionation of a species means that one molecule is oxidised while another molecule of the same species is reduced.  \nTake the **oxidation** of \\(XO_{2}\\) (reverse of (i))\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{XO_{2}(s)+2\\,H_{2}O\\;\\longrightarrow\\;XO_{4}^{-}+4\\,H^{+}+3e^{-}}\n\\tag{ox}\n\\]\n\nand the **reduction** of \\(XO_{2}\\) (reaction (ii))\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{XO_{2}(s)+4\\,H^{+}+2e^{-}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;X^{2+}+2\\,H_{2}O}\n\\tag{red}\n\\]\n\nTo make the electrons cancel, multiply (ox) by 2 and (red) by 3:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\,(ox):&\\;2XO_{2}+4H_{2}O\\;\\longrightarrow\\;2XO_{4}^{-}+8H^{+}+6e^{-}\\\\\n3\\,(red):&\\;3XO_{2}+12H^{+}+6e^{-}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;3X^{2+}+6H_{2}O\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding and cancelling the electrons gives the **balanced disproportionation reaction**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5\\,XO_{2}(s)+4\\,H^{+}\\;\\longrightarrow\\;3\\,X^{2+}+2\\,XO_{4}^{-}+2\\,H_{2}O}\n\\tag{D}\n\\]\n\nAll atoms and total charge are conserved:\n\n* X : 5 \u2192 3\u202f+\u202f2 = 5  \n* O : 5\u00b72 =10 on left; 2\u00b74\u202f+\u202f2\u00b71 =10 on right  \n* H : 4 on left; 2\u00b72 =4 on right  \n* Charge : \\(+4\\) on left, \\((+6)+(-2)=+4\\) on right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (3)  Thermodynamics of the disproportionation (D)\n\n**Cell potential**\n\nThe cathode is the reduction half\u2011reaction (ii); the anode is the oxidation half\u2011reaction (ox).  \nHence\n\n\\[\nE^{\\circ}_{\\rm cell}=E^{\\circ}_{\\rm cath}-E^{\\circ}_{\\rm anod}\n                    =E_{2}^{\\circ}-E_{1}^{\\circ}\n                    =1.200-0.900= \\boxed{+0.300\\ {\\rm V}} .\n\\]\n\n(The sign convention used here is \u201ccathode reduction potential minus anode reduction potential\u201d.)\n\n**Number of electrons transferred**\n\nIn the overall reaction the electrons that travel through the external circuit are those released in the oxidation step (3\u202fe\u207b per XO\u2082) and consumed in the reduction step (2\u202fe\u207b per XO\u2082).  \nTo obtain a balanced overall reaction we required 6\u202fe\u207b (2\u202f\u00d7\u202f3\u202fe\u207b = 3\u202f\u00d7\u202f2\u202fe\u207b).  \nThus \\(n=6\\).\n\n**Equilibrium constant**\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}K=\\frac{nE^{\\circ}_{\\rm cell}}{0.05916}\n           =\\frac{6\\times0.300}{0.05916}\n           =\\boxed{30.4}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{K=10^{30.4}\\approx 2.5\\times10^{30}} .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(K\\gg1\\), the disproportionation is overwhelmingly favoured under standard conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (4)  Dependence of \\(E_{\\rm cell}\\) on pH (activities of \\(XO_{4}^{-}\\) and \\(X^{2+}\\) fixed at 1)\n\nWrite the reaction quotient for (D):\n\n\\[\nQ=\\frac{a_{X^{2+}}^{\\,3}\\,a_{XO_{4}^{-}}^{\\,2}\\,a_{H_{2}O}^{\\,2}}\n        {a_{XO_{2}}^{\\,5}\\,a_{H^{+}}^{\\,4}} .\n\\]\n\nWith the stipulated activities \\(a_{X^{2+}}=a_{XO_{4}^{-}}=a_{XO_{2}}=1\\) and \\(a_{H_{2}O}=1\\),\n\n\\[\nQ=\\frac{1}{a_{H^{+}}^{\\,4}}=\n\\frac{1}{(10^{-pH})^{4}}=10^{4pH}.\n\\]\n\nInsert \\(Q\\) into the Nernst expression (\\(n=6\\)):\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm cell}=E^{\\circ}_{\\rm cell}-\\frac{0.05916}{6}\\log_{10}Q\n            =0.300-\\frac{0.05916}{6}(4pH)\n            =\\boxed{0.300-0.03944\\,pH\\ {\\rm V}} .\n\\]\n\n**pH at which \\(E_{\\rm cell}=0\\)**  \n\nSet the above expression to zero:\n\n\\[\n0.300-0.03944\\,pH=0\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\npH=\\frac{0.300}{0.03944}= \\boxed{7.6\\;( \\text{approximately})}.\n\\]\n\n* For **pH\u202f<\u202f7.6**\u2003\u2192\u2003\\(E_{\\rm cell}>0\\)\u2003\u2192\u2003the forward disproportionation (formation of \\(X^{2+}\\) and \\(XO_{4}^{-}\\)) is spontaneous.  \n* For **pH\u202f>\u202f7.6**\u2003\u2192\u2003\\(E_{\\rm cell}<0\\)\u2003\u2192\u2003the reverse reaction (re\u2011formation of \\(XO_{2}\\)) is favoured.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (5)  Numerical example: \\(pH=6.00,\\;a_{XO_{4}^{-}}=10^{-3},\\;a_{X^{2+}}=10^{-2}\\)\n\nFirst evaluate the reaction quotient:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ &=\\frac{(10^{-2})^{3}\\,(10^{-3})^{2}}\n          {(10^{-6})^{4}}    \\qquad\\bigl(a_{H^{+}}=10^{-pH}=10^{-6}\\bigr)\\\\\n  &=\\frac{10^{-6}\\times10^{-6}}{10^{-24}}\n   =10^{12}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\log_{10}Q = 12 .\n\\]\n\nNernst equation (again \\(n=6\\)):\n\n\\[\nE_{\\rm cell}=0.300-\\frac{0.05916}{6}\\times12\n            =0.300-0.1183\n            =\\boxed{+0.182\\ {\\rm V}} .\n\\]\n\nSince the cell potential remains positive, the disproportionation proceeds spontaneously under the specified conditions (the forward direction, i.e. formation of \\(X^{2+}\\) and \\(XO_{4}^{-}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### (6)  Why the statement \u201c\\(E_{2}^{\\circ}>E_{1}^{\\circ}\\) \u21d2 \\(XO_{2}\\) disproportionates at every pH and composition\u201d is incorrect\n\n1. **Standard potentials are not directly comparable for a coupled process.**  \n   \\(E_{2}^{\\circ}=1.20\\)\u202fV refers to the reduction of \\(XO_{2}\\) to \\(X^{2+}\\);  \n   \\(E_{1}^{\\circ}=0.90\\)\u202fV refers to the reduction of \\(XO_{4}^{-}\\) to \\(XO_{2}\\).  \n   The fact that one number is larger tells us only that, *under standard conditions*, the reduction of \\(XO_{2}\\) is more favourable than the reduction of \\(XO_{4}^{-}\\). It does **not** tell us anything about the free\u2011energy change of the *combined* disproportionation reaction.\n\n2. **The overall cell potential depends on pH and on the activities of all species.**  \n   The disproportionation cell potential is  \n   \\[\n   E_{\\rm cell}=E^{\\circ}_{\\rm cell}-\\frac{0.05916}{6}\\log_{10}Q,\n   \\qquad \n   Q=\\frac{a_{X^{2+}}^{3}a_{XO_{4}^{-}}^{2}a_{H^{+}}^{-4}}{a_{XO_{2}}^{5}} .\n   \\]  \n   As pH increases, the term \\(-\\frac{0.05916}{6}\\,4pH\\) drives \\(E_{\\rm cell}\\) negative, making the reverse reaction favourable. Therefore at sufficiently high pH the disproportionation does **not** occur.\n\n3. **Concentration (activity) effects can reverse the direction even at low pH.**  \n   In part (5) we saw that with \\(a_{X^{2+}}=10^{-2}\\) and \\(a_{XO_{4}^{-}}=10^{-3}\\) the cell potential is still positive, but if the activities were chosen differently (e.g., very large \\(a_{X^{2+}}\\) or very small \\(a_{XO_{4}^{-}}\\)) the logarithm term could outweigh the 0.300\u202fV driving force, giving a negative \\(E_{\\rm cell}\\).\n\n4. **Disproportionation is a *coupled* oxidation\u2013reduction process.**  \n   The relevant quantity is the *difference* of the two reduction potentials, \\(E^{\\circ}_{\\rm cell}=E_{2}^{\\circ}-E_{1}^{\\circ}=+0.300\\)\u202fV, not the mere ordering of the two potentials. Even this positive value can be overcome by non\u2011standard conditions.\n\nHence the student's conclusion ignores the essential role of the Nernst equation, the dependence on \\([H^{+}]\\) (pH), and the activities of the oxidised/reduced species. Disproportionation of \\(XO_{2}\\) is only guaranteed under the specific standard\u2011state conditions; it is not universally spontaneous.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer multiple subquestions.\n\nFirst, we have two half-reactions:\n\n(1) XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e- \u2192 XO2(s) + 2 H2O   E1\u00b0 = 0.900 V (reduction)\n\n(2) XO2(s) + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O    E2\u00b0 = 1.200 V (reduction)\n\nOverall reduction of XO4- to X2+ involves adding both steps: total electrons n = 5, overall reaction: XO4- + 8 H+ + 5 e- \u2192 X2+ + 4 H2O? Let's check mass balance.\n\nAdd reactions:\n\nStep1: XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e- \u2192 XO2 + 2 H2O\nStep2: XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O\n\nSum: XO4- + (4+4) H+ + (3+2) e- \u2192 X2+ + (2+2) H2O ; XO2 cancels. So overall: XO4- + 8 H+ + 5 e- \u2192 X2+ + 4 H2O. Yes.\n\nStandard cell potential for overall reduction? Use Nernst equation for overall cell: E\u00b0_overall = E\u00b0_cathode - E\u00b0_anode? Actually we want reduction potential of overall process; we can compute using weighted average based on electron numbers: E\u00b0_overall = (n1*E1\u00b0 + n2*E2\u00b0)/(n_total)? Wait careful: The overall reduction potential corresponds to the cell where the first step is cathodic (reduction) and second also reduction but they are sequential. However you can treat them as series; the overall E\u00b0 should be sum of potentials weighted by number of electrons? In electrochemistry, when combining half-reactions, the overall cell potential is not simple arithmetic mean; it's determined from Gibbs free energy: \u0394G\u00b0 = -n_total * F * E\u00b0_overall = \u03a3 \u0394G_i\u00b0 = -n_i * F * E_i\u00b0. Therefore E\u00b0_overall = (\u03a3 n_i E_i\u00b0)/n_total. This is a weighted average, not simple mean.\n\nCompute: n1=3, E1=0.900 V => contribution = 2.700 V\u00b7e. n2=2, E2=1.200 V => contribution = 2.400 V\u00b7e. Sum = 5.100 V\u00b7e. Divide by n_total=5 gives E\u00b0_overall = 1.02 V.\n\nThus overall five-electron reduction potential = 1.020 V.\n\nExplain why cannot just average (0.9+1.2)/2 = 1.05 V because weighting matters due to different electron counts.\n\nNow (2): Determine if XO2(s) is thermodynamically unstable toward disproportionation into XO4- and X2+. Disproportionation means a single species undergoes simultaneous oxidation and reduction to give higher and lower oxidation states. Here XO2 (+IV) could disproportionate to XO4 (+VII) (oxidation) and X2+ (+II) (reduction). Write half-reactions:\n\nOxidation (reverse of reaction 1): XO2(s) \u2192 XO4- + 2 H2O + 3 e- - 4 H+? Actually reverse of reduction: XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e-. But we need to include water correctly. Starting from reduction: XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e- \u2192 XO2 + 2 H2O. Reverse: XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e-.\n\nReduction (reverse of reaction 2): X2+ + 2 H2O \u2192 XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e-. Wait original reduction: XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O. Reverse: X2+ + 2 H2O \u2192 XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e-.\n\nBut for disproportionation we need net reaction where XO2 is both oxidized to XO4- and reduced to X2+. So combine oxidation half (XO2 -> XO4-) providing electrons, and reduction half (X2+ -> XO2) consuming electrons. However we need to start with some amount of XO2 only, no X2+ initially. So we need to multiply half-reactions appropriately such that electrons cancel and XO2 appears on both sides.\n\nLet's write oxidation half (O): XO2 \u2192 XO4- + 2 H2O + 3 e- - 4 H+? Actually from reversed reaction: XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e-. That's Oxi: XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e-.\n\nReduction half (R): X2+ + 2 H2O \u2192 XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e-.\n\nMultiply R by 3 to get 6 e- consumed, O by 2 to get 6 e- produced.\n\n2 O: 2 XO2 + 4 H2O \u2192 2 XO4- + 8 H+ + 6 e-\n3 R: 3 X2+ + 6 H2O \u2192 3 XO2 + 12 H+ + 6 e-\n\nAdd: Left: 2 XO2 + 4 H2O + 3 X2+ + 6 H2O = 2 XO2 + 3 X2+ + 10 H2O\nRight: 2 XO4- + 8 H+ + 3 XO2 + 12 H+ = 3 XO2 + 2 XO4- + 20 H+\n\nCancel common XO2 (both sides 2?). Right side has 3 XO2, left side 2 XO2, leaving net XO2 on right side. Let's subtract 2 XO2 from both sides: left becomes 3 X2+ + 10 H2O, right becomes XO2 + 2 XO4- + 20 H+. Also water may be adjusted.\n\nBetter approach: Since we want disproportionation of XO2 alone, we need to eliminate X2+ from reactants. So we can consider net reaction where a certain number of XO2 molecules produce XO4- and X2+, maybe plus/minus water and H+. Let's attempt to find smallest integer coefficients.\n\nSet oxidation half (O): XO2 \u2192 XO4- + 2 H2O + 3 e- - 4 H+? Actually we need to keep charge balanced. Original reduction had charges: XO4- (-1), 4 H+ (+4), 3 e- (-3) \u2192 XO2 (neutral) + 2 H2O (neutral). Balanced. Reverse: XO2 (0) + 2 H2O (0) \u2192 XO4- (-1) + 4 H+ (+4) + 3 e- (-3). Charges: left 0, right -1+4-3=0 good.\n\nReduction half (R) reverse: X2+ (+2) + 2 H2O (0) \u2192 XO2 (0) + 4 H+ (+4) + 2 e- (-2). Charge left +2, right +4-2=+2 good.\n\nNow combine O (produces electrons) and R (consumes electrons). Multiply O by 2 (to get 6 e-) and R by 3 (to get 6 e-). Add:\n\n2 O: 2 XO2 + 4 H2O \u2192 2 XO4- + 8 H+ + 6 e-\n3 R: 3 X2+ + 6 H2O \u2192 3 XO2 + 12 H+ + 6 e-\n\nAdd: left: 2 XO2 + 4 H2O + 3 X2+ + 6 H2O = 2 XO2 + 3 X2+ + 10 H2O\nright: 2 XO4- + 8 H+ + 3 XO2 + 12 H+ = 3 XO2 + 2 XO4- + 20 H+\n\nSubtract 2 XO2 from both sides yields net:\n\nLeft: 3 X2+ + 10 H2O\nRight: XO2 + 2 XO4- + 20 H+\n\nSo net reaction: 3 X^{2+} + 10 H2O \u2192 XO2(s) + 2 XO4- + 20 H+ ?\n\nBut that's not disproportionation of XO2; it consumes X2+. Not correct.\n\nMaybe we need to set up disproportionation where XO2 acts as both oxidant and reductant, meaning we need to combine oxidation half (XO2 \u2192 XO4-) with reduction half (XO2 \u2192 X2+)? Wait reduction half is X2+ being reduced to XO2, not oxidation. To have XO2 reduce something else, we need another species to be oxidized. But disproportionation of a single species means one molecule is oxidized while another molecule of same species is reduced. So we need to have both oxidation and reduction of XO2 simultaneously: one XO2 goes to XO4- (oxidized) producing electrons, another XO2 goes to X2+ (reduced) consuming electrons. That suggests we need to reverse reaction 2 (which reduces XO2 to X2+) but we need oxidation of XO2 to X2+? Actually reaction 2 reduces XO2 to X2+, i.e., XO2 gains electrons. For disproportionation, we need XO2 to lose electrons (oxidized) to become XO4- and also gain electrons (reduced) to become X2+. So we need to use oxidation half (reverse of reaction 1) and reduction half (original reaction 2) but with opposite direction? Wait original reaction 2 is reduction of XO2 to X2+, which matches the reduction part needed for disproportionation (XO2 being reduced). Good. So we keep reaction 2 as written (reduction). For oxidation part, we need XO2 being oxidized to XO4-, which is the reverse of reaction 1. So we will add reaction 2 (as given) and reverse of reaction 1, then multiply to balance electrons.\n\nLet's denote:\n\nReductive half (R): XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O   (E2\u00b0)\n\nOxidative half (O_rev): XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e-   (reverse of reaction 1)\n\nNow combine: Need electrons equal. Multiply R by 3 (6 e-) and O_rev by 2 (6 e-).\n\n3 R: 3 XO2 + 12 H+ + 6 e- \u2192 3 X2+ + 6 H2O\n2 O_rev: 2 XO2 + 4 H2O \u2192 2 XO4- + 8 H+ + 6 e-\n\nAdd: left: 3 XO2 + 12 H+ + 2 XO2 + 4 H2O = 5 XO2 + 12 H+ + 4 H2O\nright: 3 X2+ + 6 H2O + 2 XO4- + 8 H+ = 3 X2+ + 2 XO4- + 6 H2O + 8 H+\n\nNow combine waters: left 4 H2O, right 6 H2O, move 4 H2O to right subtract: left 5 XO2 + 12 H+, right 3 X2+ + 2 XO4- + 2 H2O + 8 H+. Cancel 12 H+ vs 8 H+: left 12 H+, right 8 H+, move 8 H+ to left: left 5 XO2 + 12 H+ - 8 H+ = 5 XO2 + 4 H+. Right: 3 X2+ + 2 XO4- + 2 H2O.\n\nThus net: 5 XO2 + 4 H+ \u2192 3 X2+ + 2 XO4- + 2 H2O.\n\nCheck atom balance: X: left 5, right 3+2=5 ok. O: left from XO2 each has 2 O, so 5*2=10 O. Plus H+ none O. Right: 2 XO4- each 4 O => 8 O, plus 2 H2O => 2 O, total 10 O. H: left 4 H+ => 4 H. Right: 2 H2O => 4 H. Good. Charge: left: 4 H+ => +4. Right: 3 X2+ => +6, 2 XO4- => -2, total +4. Balanced.\n\nThus disproportionation reaction: 5 XO2(s) + 4 H+ \u2192 3 X^{2+} + 2 XO4^- + 2 H2O.\n\nAlternatively can divide by GCD? No common factor. So that's the balanced disproportionation.\n\nNow (3): Determine E\u00b0_cell for this reaction. Cell potential = E\u00b0_cathode - E\u00b0_anode? Which half-reaction is reduction (cathode) and which is oxidation (anode). In our net reaction, we have both reduction and oxidation of XO2. We can think of coupling the two half-reactions we used: reduction half (R) is XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O (E2\u00b0 = 1.200 V). Oxidation half (O_rev) is XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e- (E\u00b0_ox = -E\u00b0_red of original reaction 1). Original reaction 1 reduction potential E1\u00b0 = 0.900 V. So oxidation potential = -0.900 V.\n\nWhen constructing cell, we pair reduction half with oxidation half. The cell potential is E\u00b0_cell = E\u00b0_reduction (cathode) - E\u00b0_oxidation (anode) ??? Actually E\u00b0_cell = E\u00b0_cathode (reduction) - E\u00b0_anode (reduction potential). Since anode reaction is oxidation, its reduction potential is E\u00b0_ox (the reduction potential of the reverse reaction). For oxidation half, the reduction potential is E1\u00b0 = 0.900 V. So E\u00b0_cell = E2\u00b0 - E1\u00b0 = 1.200 - 0.900 = 0.300 V. However note we multiplied by different numbers of electrons; but cell potential independent of scaling. Indeed the net reaction's cell potential equals difference between potentials of the two half-reactions (since they involve different electron numbers, but the Nernst equation uses n_total). Let's verify using \u0394G\u00b0 method: \u0394G\u00b0_cell = -n_total F E\u00b0_cell = \u03a3 \u03bd_i \u0394G_i\u00b0. Compute contributions: For reduction half (R) with n=2, \u0394G\u00b0_R = -2F E2\u00b0 = -2F*1.200 = -2.400 F. For oxidation half (O_rev) which is reverse of reaction 1, its \u0394G\u00b0_ox = + (\u0394G\u00b0_red of reaction1) = +3F*0.900 = +2.700 F (since reversing changes sign). Total \u0394G\u00b0 = -2.400F + 2.700F = +0.300F. Thus E\u00b0_cell = -\u0394G\u00b0/ (n_total F) = -(+0.300F)/(5F) = -0.060 V? Wait n_total = 5 electrons transferred overall (from earlier). Using \u0394G\u00b0 = -n_total F E\u00b0_cell. Solve: -5F E\u00b0_cell = +0.300F => E\u00b0_cell = -0.060 V. Hmm discrepancy. Let's re-evaluate signs.\n\nOur net reaction: 5 XO2 + 4 H+ \u2192 3 X2+ + 2 XO4- + 2 H2O. According to our construction, we added reduction half (R) times 3 and oxidation half (O_rev) times 2. The overall electrons transferred cancel (6 each). The net \u0394G\u00b0 = \u03a3 \u03bd_i \u0394G_i\u00b0, where each half-reaction's \u0394G\u00b0 uses its own n.\n\nCalculate \u0394G\u00b0 for each half-reaction (as written in forward direction as given originally):\n\nReaction 1 (reduction): XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e- \u2192 XO2 + 2 H2O, \u0394G1\u00b0 = -3F * E1\u00b0 = -3F*0.900 = -2.700 F.\n\nReaction 2 (reduction): XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O, \u0394G2\u00b0 = -2F * E2\u00b0 = -2F*1.200 = -2.400 F.\n\nNow oxidation half is reverse of reaction 1: XO2 + 2 H2O \u2192 XO4- + 4 H+ + 3 e-, \u0394G_Ox = +2.700 F.\n\nReduction half is reaction 2 as written: XO2 + 4 H+ + 2 e- \u2192 X2+ + 2 H2O, \u0394G_R = -2.400 F.\n\nWe used 3 copies of reduction (R) and 2 copies of oxidation (Ox). So total \u0394G_total = 3*(-2.400 F) + 2*(+2.700 F) = -7.200 F + 5.400 F = -1.800 F.\n\nThus \u0394G_total = -1.800 F. Number of electrons transferred overall in net reaction? When we combine, electrons cancel; net reaction does not involve electrons (they are internal). For cell potential calculation via \u0394G = -n_total F E\u00b0, we need n_total = total electrons transferred in the cell (i.e., the number that would flow through external circuit if the reaction were separated). In our constructed cell, the electrons from oxidation travel to reduction; the total number of electrons per overall reaction is the least common multiple of the individual electron counts divided by the coefficient after cancellation? A